You're on **.NET 10** — the best approach is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package. It wraps `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and more behind an `IFileSystem` interface with a ready-made mock for tests.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI (Program.cs / Startup.cs)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new ConfigService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));
service.Write("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
Assert.True(service.DirectoryExists("/data"));
```

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` is battle-tested, covers the full `System.IO` surface, and the `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem out of the box — no hand-rolling needed.
